      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous text (<htcite0>), we divided a large rectangle into 10 smaller ones, all of the same size (**Graph 1**). In doing so, we were already limiting the size of the rectangles we use.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f71fa183-033d-437c-9bde-5ffb31e296d7",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Continuity",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg width=\"450\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"> <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <line x1=\"2\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <text x=\"0\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0</text> <text x=\"41\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">1</text> <text x=\"82\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">2</text> <text x=\"123\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">3</text> <text x=\"164\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">4</text> <text x=\"205\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">5</text> <text x=\"246\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">6</text> <text x=\"287\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">7</text> <text x=\"328\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">8</text> <text x=\"369\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">9</text> <text x=\"405\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">10</text> <line x1=\"3\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"3\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"44\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"126\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"126\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"167\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"167\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"208\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"208\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"249\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"249\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"331\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"331\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"372\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"372\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"413\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"413\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"43\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"84\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"125\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"166\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"207\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"248\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"289\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"330\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"370\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Representation of 10 rectangles along a path.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: A Common Rule</b><br /><br />When studying limits in Volume 1 of the <i>First Steps</i> section, we saw that there is no maximum value: we can always add one number to another, making them tend toward infinity (<htcite0>).<br /><br />If we keep dividing a rectangle into 10 smaller ones, is there any limit to how far we can divide?</div></div></center>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural Limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "02"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_pieces",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Kitchen Through Time\n\nWalk into any kitchen on the planet today and you will find objects used to prepare food, as in the Shona kitchen (**Figure 1**) (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1c6f4dce-a58a-4c06-b4a8-6f509575f42a",
                     "text": "Muratu, Brian Z.",
                     "page": "pp. 56",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgShona\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgShona', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Photos of a Shona kitchen. In the top left, we see a photo of the kitchen with the door closed. In the top right, we see the back of the kitchen. In the bottom section, there are images of the interior of the structure. These photos were taken by Brian Z. Muratu (<htcite0>) and are being used under the terms defined by the University of Johannesburg, which shared the dissertation under the <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)</a> license.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1c6f4dce-a58a-4c06-b4a8-6f509575f42a",
                     "text": "Muratu, Brian Z.",
                     "page": "pp. 56",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Or also in a Maya kitchen (**Figure 2**):",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"JC\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('JC', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 2</b>: Ceramic bowls found at Joya de Cerén, El Salvador. Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "24"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Some Maya ceramic pieces have survived to the present day in a good state of preservation for various reasons. At Joya de Cerén, for example, a volcanic eruption contributed to the preservation of some objects (<htcite0>). However, we cannot consider this a general rule. For instance, some ceramic pieces from the 11th to the 13th centuries, according to the Gregorian calendar, have not survived intact to our time (**Figure 3**) (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "21315015-e589-486f-b0aa-3eba1d775b1d",
                     "text": "Ministerio de la Cultura, El Salvador",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f7381734-81dc-4bef-8527-44317f40c4b4",
                     "text": "Zhao, B.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2015",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img class=\"imgcenter\" id=\"imgBZ0\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgBZ0', '0%')\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: Jingdezhen kiln complex and southern China, 11th to early 13th century, proven from the Sanje ya Kati site, Kilwa-Masoko, Tanzania. All rights reserved to Dr. Bing Zhou, who has kindly permitted the use of these photographs for educational purposes.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This scenario allows us to conclude that, over time, objects also fragment due to natural processes.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_division",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Modeling the Interior\n\nOur goal now is to create the conditions to measure smaller values between the integers shown in **Graph 1**. To do this, we will turn again to our hands, the ones we used to learn to count in Volume 1 of the *First Steps* section (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>).\n\n#### A First Step\n\nJust as we used our fingers to create the numbers from 0 to 9, we will use ten again to divide the intervals: first, ten rectangles between the numbers 0 and 1 (**Graph 2**).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "cc8c1467-a6af-4f9c-ba60-ab6df35aba05",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Beyond fingers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9618a83e-f232-49db-84f6-fc3455a3f1d1",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Drawing to count",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "648fb7b4-ba73-452e-980b-6d68d57d16b9",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Hindu-Arabic Numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg width=\"450\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"> <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <line x1=\"2\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <text x=\"0\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0</text> <text x=\"405\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">1</text> <line x1=\"3\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"3\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"44\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"126\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"126\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"167\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"167\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"208\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"208\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"249\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"249\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"331\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"331\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"372\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"372\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"413\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"413\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"43\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"84\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"125\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"166\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"207\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"248\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"289\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"330\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"370\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: Representation of 10 rectangles on a path within the reduced interval.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: And now?</b><br /><br />How would you fill in values between 0 and 1?<br /><br />Could your first answer also work with the numbers between 1 and 2?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Dividing\n\nWe first studied how numbers relate to one another in Volume 1. There, we saw that numbers can be grouped into numerical families: inside each family, the digit on the left always carries the largest value (<htcite0>).\n\nFollowing that idea, we will use the digit on the left to show where a number sits in an order, and we will keep decimal digits to name the values between 0 and 1.\n\nWhen we write numbers between zero and one, we cannot simply place one digit next to another: it would be confused with the numbers already shown in **Graph 1**, or it would change a number's place in the order. So we will adopt a notation that marks a number as lying between two others instead of making it look greater than them. Let's see how it looks in **Graph 3**.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "00f3ab60-2017-420a-99dc-56de384243e8",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Numerical families (Part I)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg width=\"450\" height=\"140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"black\"> <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <line x1=\"2\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"440\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <text x=\"0\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.0</text> <text x=\"35\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.1</text> <text x=\"76\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.2</text> <text x=\"117\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.3</text> <text x=\"158\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.4</text> <text x=\"199\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.5</text> <text x=\"240\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.6</text> <text x=\"281\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.7</text> <text x=\"322\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.8</text> <text x=\"363\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">0.9</text> <text x=\"400\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"1.5em\">1.0</text> <line x1=\"3\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"3\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"44\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"85\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"126\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"126\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"167\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"167\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"208\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"208\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"249\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"249\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"290\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"331\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"331\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"372\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"372\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <line x1=\"413\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"413\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" /> <rect x=\"2\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"43\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"84\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"125\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>  <rect x=\"166\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"207\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"248\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"289\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"330\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/> <rect x=\"370\" y=\"2\" width=\"40\" height=\"95\" fill=\"lightblue\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: Representation of 10 rectangles on a path showing decimal numbers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Compare **Graphs 1 and 3** to see how the numbers differ. We can highlight the following:\n- The range is different: **Graph 1** shows intervals from 0 to 10, while **Graph 3** presents values between 0 and 1.\n- The values in **Graph 1** are whole numbers (integers): they have no separator—the dot (.)—that we just introduced.\n- **Graph 3**, in contrast, holds numbers whose dot separates the whole value (on the left) from the part that lies between the integers (on the right).\n- As the digits to the right of the dot grow toward 9, we move closer to the next whole number.\n- As they shrink toward zero, we move back toward the whole number on the left of the dot.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we kept studying how to fill the gaps between numbers, moving us closer to a more accurate description of the universe. We have not yet finished explaining how numbers are written between whole numbers; the next text will take the next step in that direction.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Do numbers exist between whole numbers?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "Besides the numbers we use for counting, there are also numbers that lie between them; they help us, among other things, describe the universe more precisely."
      },
      {
